[{:seon.task/name "a-cached-read-records-what-it-read"
  :seon.task/plan "runtime-2's order for Datahike Pin B (2026-11-15): recording must never switch a cache off, and a hit replays the ranges its computation read. What exists [M, read]: datahike.db.search/memoize-for (db/search.cljc:20) bypasses the search memo whenever di/*reads* is bound, and datahike.reads/with-reads binds q/*query-result-cache?* false, so every recorded read (a reaction's run, a render pass) recomputes what an unrecorded one serves from cache (q over 680 tasks: 10 us cached, 1,158 us recorded, root's db [M]). The search memo is off in Seon (:cache-size nil on root [M, 2026-11-15]), so its bypass costs nothing here and it stays as upstream has it. Change, the q result cache: its entry carries the ranges its computation read (computed under a recorder of its own on a miss); a hit while recording adds them to the bound recorder; with-reads stops switching the cache off. Estimate: a cache miss + the ranges its run records (a set conj each, ~200 ticks, ~150 B kept a range) against the query's own work; a recorded hit replays its ranges, O(ranges of the entry), typically 1-3, instead of the search; off the recorder a hit is unchanged. Proof, red first: a recorded q the result cache already holds records the same keys as an uncached one, and is served by the cache. See docs/storage.md#what-a-read-scanned."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.reads-test/a-cached-read-records-what-it-read}}]
